  <h2>The fact</h2>

  <p>
    PyTorch's <code>torch.compile</code> hits a no-op recompilation cliff if you
    pass tensors with the same shape but different stride layouts in the same
    call.
  </p>

  <h2>The proof</h2>

  <pre><code>import torch

@torch.compile
def f(x):
    return x.sum()

a = torch.randn(64, 64).contiguous()
b = a.t()  # same shape, transposed strides
print(f(a))  # compiles
print(f(b))  # *recompiles* — different stride pattern</code></pre>

  <p>
    Setting <code>TORCHDYNAMO_VERBOSE=1</code> shows the recompilation event;
    counting them with <code>torch._dynamo.utils.compile_times()</code> confirms.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>

  <p>
    For training loops that mix <code>x</code> and <code>x.transpose(...)</code>,
    you'll silently double compile cost. Cure is either:
    <code>x = x.contiguous()</code> at the boundary, or marking the dim as
    dynamic via <code>torch._dynamo.mark_dynamic</code>.
  </p>

  <p class="footnote">
    Filed under: things the docs mention but you only learn by getting bitten.
  </p>
